Calls.Text:
React components normally re-render when their parent re-renders, even if the child’s props appear unchanged. If you pass an object literal, it is also a **new object reference on every render**:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return <Child options={{ theme: "dark" }} />;
}
```

Each render creates a new `{ theme: "dark" }` object:

```js
{ theme: "dark" } !== { theme: "dark" } // true
```

This matters when `Child` uses `React.memo`, because `memo` performs a shallow comparison of props. The new reference makes `options` look changed.

### Fix with `React.memo` and `useMemo`

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{options.theme}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = useMemo(() => ({ theme: "dark" }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>
      <Child options={options} />
    </>
  );
}
```

Or, preferably, pass primitive values when practical:

```jsx
<Child theme="dark" />
```

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ theme }) {
  return <div>{theme}</div>;
});
```

The same issue applies to inline arrays and functions:

```jsx
<Child items={[1, 2, 3]} onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Use `useMemo` for arrays/objects and `useCallback` for functions when stable identity is actually needed.

Also note:

- `React.memo` only prevents re-renders caused by unchanged parent props.
- The child still re-renders when its own state or consumed context changes.
- In development, `StrictMode` may intentionally invoke renders more than once to detect side effects.

