Calls.Text:
React re-renders a component whenever its **own state changes**, regardless of whether its props changed. Also, when a parent re-renders, its children normally re-render too.

Passing an object can make memoization ineffective because objects are compared by **reference**, not by contents:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return <Child options={{ theme: "dark" }} />;
}
```

Each parent render creates a new object:

```js
{ theme: "dark" } !== { theme: "dark" }
```

So even if `Child` is wrapped in `React.memo`, React sees `options` as changed.

### Stabilize the object reference

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const options = useMemo(() => ({ theme: "dark" }), []);

  return <Child options={options} />;
}

const Child = React.memo(function Child({ options }) {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{options.theme}</div>;
});
```

If the object depends on values, include them in the dependency array:

```jsx
const options = useMemo(
  () => ({ theme, pageSize }),
  [theme, pageSize]
);
```

Alternatively, define constant objects outside the component when they never change:

```jsx
const OPTIONS = { theme: "dark" };
```

Also note:

- `React.memo` is required if you want a child to skip renders when props are unchanged.
- Functions passed as props have the same identity issue; use `useCallback` when appropriate.
- Context changes cause consumers to re-render even with `React.memo`.
- If the state being updated belongs to the component itself, that component is expected to re-render.
- In development, React Strict Mode may intentionally invoke renders more than once to detect side effects.

